{
  "description": "NFX-10 §1 STUN server selection. In 'cases', 'own' are the client's own servers; 'beacons' are [signer, address] pairs, in the order heard, from the stun endpoints of the live, verified beacons of the video; 'creator' is the video's creator, the key in those beacons' 'a' tag and the manifest's author; 'announced' are [operator, address] pairs of live announcements for the network. 'selected' is what the client uses, in order: its own first, each once; then the addresses of the beacons the creator signed, in the order heard, each once, that are global unicast in canonical form, on a port of 1024 or above, and not its own; at most 'max' in all. A beacon signed by any other key adds nothing, however many keys name a server, and an announced server is never taken, whoever signed it. 'global_unicast' lists addresses a client may and may not take from Nostr. Global unicast here also excludes the documentation blocks (192.0.2.0/24, 198.51.100.0/24, 203.0.113.0/24, 2001:db8::/32, 3fff::/20), which route nowhere; beacons that name them in a stun endpoint stay valid beacons (beacon-stun.json), and only this selection refuses them. 'fallback' is what a client may add when no server it asks ('in_use', its own included; none at all is allowed) gave a mapping ('mapped' false), whether or not it has servers of its own ('own'): in the order of 'beacons' ([seeder, address] of live verified beacons of the video, the creator's included), at most one server per seeder, global unicast, on a port of 1024 or above, not asked yet, whose IP address is in 'signaling' (the addresses of the candidates of the peers it is signaling with), while it asks fewer than 'max' in all. 'announced' servers are never taken. No traffic is ever sent to an address of a vector.",
